Ddoc

$(SPEC_S Unit Tests,

$(HEADERNAV_TOC)

$(P_LASER_D Laser-D does not include D's built-in unit-test framework. Tests
are ordinary Laser-D programs with explicit functions, explicit calls, and one
of the supported C entry points.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 declarations, Unit-Test Declarations))

$(LASER_REJECTED The $(D unittest) block declaration is rejected at module,
aggregate, and template scope. It is rejected regardless of compiler options;
the compiler never silently skips its contents.)

$(P A test should instead be written as an ordinary function and invoked
explicitly from:

$(D_CODE
extern(C) int main()
{
    return runTests();
}
)

This keeps control flow visible and gives tests the same language and runtime
model as other Laser-D programs.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 enabling, Compiler Option and Version))

$(LASER_REJECTED The $(D -unittest) compiler option is rejected. The predefined
$(D unittest) version condition is never enabled by the Laser-D compiler.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 discovery, Unit-Test Discovery))

$(LASER_REJECTED $(D __traits(getUnitTests, symbol)) is rejected. Laser-D does
not generate hidden unit-test functions or provide a reflection protocol for
discovering them.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 execution, Execution Model))

$(P Laser-D performs no automatic test registration or execution before
$(D main). Test ordering, result reporting, and failure propagation are
implemented explicitly by the test program or its C-compatible test harness.
This requires neither ModuleInfo nor druntime.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 assertions, Assertions))

$(P Runtime $(D assert) expressions remain supported independently of the
rejected built-in unit-test framework. Ordinary explicit test programs may use
mandatory runtime assertions. Compiler-only $(D static assert) declarations
also remain supported.)

$(SPEC_SUBNAV_PREV_NEXT errors, Error Handling, garbage, Garbage Collection)
)

Macros:
    CHAPTER=28
        TITLE=Unit Tests
        _=
